# The divisor power function and the square power it samples. The divisor power function sigma-k of a number is the sum of
# the k-th powers of its divisors, and it is MULTIPLICATIVE: on coprime numbers sigma-k of the product is the product of
# the sigma-k values. The k-th powers it sums have exact gnomon structure, and this file MODELS the square, the k equals
# two case, by its GNOMON: the difference between consecutive squares is the odd number two n minus one, so the squares
# are the running sums of the odd numbers. It DEFINES the square by accumulating those odd increments and PROVES the
# accumulation is exactly n squared, for every n, so the square is pinned by its gnomon. It then records, as machine-
# integer facts, the multiplicativity of the divisor sum on a coprime pair and its value on a prime. The gnomon closed
# form is proved for every n by the commutative-ring normalizer.

# The square of n, built by accumulating the odd gnomons: square(0) = 0, and square(n) = square(n-1) + (2n - 1), the odd
# increment from one square to the next.
task square
  take n, like natural-number
  like natural-number
  send back
    fork test
      hook test
        call is-equal
          read n
          code 0
      hook hold
        code 0
      hook miss
        call add
          call square
            call subtract
              read n
              code 1
          call add
            call multiply
              code 2
              call subtract
                read n
                code 1
            code 1

# THE GNOMON CLOSED FORM: accumulating the odd numbers gives exactly the squares, square(n) = n * n, for every n. So the
# square is determined by its gnomon, the odd numbers, the discrete derivative of n squared. Proved for all n by the
# commutative-ring normalizer.
rule the-odd-gnomons-accumulate-to-the-square
  seat n, like natural-number
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call square
        read n
      call multiply
        read n
        read n
  fold n

# THE DIVISOR SUM IS MULTIPLICATIVE on coprime numbers, as machine-integer facts. The divisors of six are 1, 2, 3, 6
# summing to twelve, and six is two times three with two and three coprime, so sigma(6) = sigma(2) * sigma(3) = 3 * 4 = 12.
hold divisor-sum-is-multiplicative-at-six
  call is-equal
    code 12
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 4

# THE DIVISOR SUM OF A PRIME is the prime plus one, its only divisors being one and itself: sigma(5) = 1 + 5 = 6.
hold divisor-sum-of-five-is-six
  call is-equal
    code 6
    call add
      code 1
      code 5
